      "anchor": "normalization-casimir-descent",
      "title": "Why opposite-sign lowering must stop",
      "hypotheses": "The ordered target has gaps at least τ. Put R=2S, s=√(2S) and suppose R<J[2(τ−2(s+J))−2n−2] for a positive integer J.",
      "statement": "Fewer than J lowerings reach an irreducible compact type with no lower first-layer highest vector and with coordinate error ≤s+J. With the stated larger gap the corresponding regular parameter differs from the target by at most s+J+n in each coordinate.",
